# Let the load balancer decide whether an instance is alive.
#
# Elastic Beanstalk creates the Auto Scaling group with HealthCheckType: EC2,
# which only asks the hypervisor whether the machine is running. On 2026-09-11
# a deployment wedged the instance: the Node process stopped answering, the ALB
# marked the target unhealthy, and the API was down for forty minutes - while
# the ASG went on reporting the instance "Healthy", because as far as EC2 was
# concerned the box was fine. Nothing was ever going to replace it.
#
# With ELB health checks the ASG uses the same verdict the load balancer already
# reached, so an instance whose application is dead gets replaced automatically.
#
# The grace period is deliberately long. A new instance has to boot, run
# `npm --omit=dev install`, start Node and connect to Mongo before it can pass a
# health check, and an impatient grace period turns a slow start into a
# replacement loop that never converges. Fifteen minutes is far more than the
# ~40 seconds a healthy launch actually takes, and the cost of being generous
# here is only that a genuinely dead instance lives a few minutes longer.
#
# This lives in .ebextensions rather than being set on the ASG by hand because
# Elastic Beanstalk owns that resource: a manual change is liable to be reverted
# the next time EB rebuilds or reconfigures the group, and it would vanish
# silently. Declared here, it is version-controlled and reapplied on every
# deployment.
Resources:
  AWSEBAutoScalingGroup:
    Type: AWS::AutoScaling::AutoScalingGroup
    Properties:
      HealthCheckType: ELB
      HealthCheckGracePeriod: 900
